      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stevensville is officially recognized as the first permanent settlement of non‑indigenous peoples in Montana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stevensville%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1841, Jesuit missionaries founded Mary's Mission and Fort Owen, whose surviving structures still stand in present‑day Stevensville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stevensville%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "More than forty properties in Stevensville are listed on the National Register of Historic Places, showcasing its rich architectural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stevensville%2C_Montana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Bitterroot Valley, where Stevensville sits, is the ancestral homeland of the Bitterroot Salish people.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stevensville%2C_Montana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every summer, the town hosts Western Heritage Days, featuring parades and lively reenactments that celebrate its frontier past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitstevensville.com/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
